1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,888
Reading a table is only half the job.

2
00:00:02,888 --> 00:00:08,125
You also create it, fill it, change it, and sometimes throw a column away.

3
00:00:08,125 --> 00:00:10,555
Until now the table felt like a given.

4
00:00:10,555 --> 00:00:13,305
Someone else had already named the columns.

5
00:00:13,305 --> 00:00:14,722
Now you are the someone.

6
00:00:14,722 --> 00:00:18,805
CREATE TABLE names the table and lists columns with types.

7
00:00:18,805 --> 00:00:22,138
Each column is one attribute you promise to store.

8
00:00:22,138 --> 00:00:25,000
customer_id is an integer.

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,347
name is text.

10
00:00:26,347 --> 00:00:27,819
city is text.

11
00:00:27,819 --> 00:00:29,625
That list is a contract.

12
00:00:29,625 --> 00:00:32,000
Rows that arrive later have to fit it.

13
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:33,597
INSERT adds a row.

14
00:00:33,597 --> 00:00:37,680
You list the columns, then the values, in the same order.

15
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:42,291
A second INSERT is a second customer — not an edit of the first.

16
00:00:42,291 --> 00:00:43,722
Maya is one row.

17
00:00:43,722 --> 00:00:45,111
Jordan is another.

18
00:00:45,111 --> 00:00:50,027
If you insert Maya twice, you now have two Mayas unless a key stops you.

19
00:00:50,027 --> 00:00:53,138
UPDATE changes values already in the table.

20
00:00:53,138 --> 00:00:55,111
SET names the new value.

21
00:00:55,111 --> 00:00:57,083
WHERE names which rows move.

22
00:00:57,083 --> 00:01:00,972
Always pair UPDATE with WHERE, or every row moves.

23
00:01:00,972 --> 00:01:02,916
That is the classic accident.

24
00:01:02,916 --> 00:01:04,625
You meant Sofia's city.

25
00:01:04,625 --> 00:01:06,541
You rewrote the whole column.

26
00:01:06,541 --> 00:01:08,486
DELETE removes rows.

27
00:01:08,486 --> 00:01:11,972
Again: WHERE, or the table goes quiet.

28
00:01:11,972 --> 00:01:15,861
DELETE FROM customers with no filter is not bravery.

29
00:01:15,861 --> 00:01:17,027
It is a wipe.

30
00:01:17,027 --> 00:01:19,236
SELECT first when you are unsure.

31
00:01:19,236 --> 00:01:21,375
See the rows the WHERE would catch.

32
00:01:21,375 --> 00:01:22,513
Then change them.

33
00:01:22,513 --> 00:01:26,125
ALTER TABLE adds a column when the world changes.

34
00:01:26,125 --> 00:01:28,069
Maybe you now store an email.

35
00:01:28,069 --> 00:01:31,833
Existing rows get NULL unless you supply a default.

36
00:01:31,833 --> 00:01:34,083
ALTER can also rename a column.

37
00:01:34,083 --> 00:01:35,527
It can drop a column.

38
00:01:35,527 --> 00:01:38,402
Dropping a column throws the values away.

39
00:01:38,402 --> 00:01:41,375
There is no undo in the statement itself.

40
00:01:41,375 --> 00:01:44,222
DROP TABLE removes the table itself.

41
00:01:44,222 --> 00:01:45,902
That is not a row delete.

42
00:01:45,902 --> 00:01:47,625
The whole object is gone.

43
00:01:47,625 --> 00:01:50,638
The name, the columns, the rows.

44
00:01:50,638 --> 00:01:52,833
Think of CREATE as the shape.

45
00:01:52,833 --> 00:01:54,958
INSERT as the first facts.

46
00:01:54,958 --> 00:01:56,666
UPDATE as a correction.

47
00:01:56,666 --> 00:01:59,166
DELETE as a removal of a fact.

48
00:01:59,166 --> 00:02:01,666
ALTER as a later change to the shape.

49
00:02:01,666 --> 00:02:04,263
DROP as taking the object off the shelf.

50
00:02:04,263 --> 00:02:07,666
Practice on a copy, not the only copy you have.

51
00:02:07,666 --> 00:02:09,388
Insert two rows.

52
00:02:09,388 --> 00:02:10,486
Update one.

53
00:02:10,486 --> 00:02:11,722
Delete the other.

54
00:02:11,722 --> 00:02:13,361
Select to see the shape.

55
00:02:13,361 --> 00:02:15,736
If ALTER feels scary, it should.

56
00:02:15,736 --> 00:02:19,000
You are changing the contract other queries depend on.

57
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,430
Write the dangerous statements slowly.

58
00:02:21,430 --> 00:02:23,152
Read the WHERE twice.

59
00:02:23,152 --> 00:02:25,722
Then the table stays a place you can trust.

60
00:02:25,722 --> 00:02:31,402
Transactions belong with this family of verbs, even if they get their own later lesson.

61
00:02:31,402 --> 00:02:36,847
BEGIN, then INSERT, then a SELECT that looks wrong, then ROLLBACK.

62
00:02:36,847 --> 00:02:38,833
The table forgets the insert.

63
00:02:38,833 --> 00:02:40,430
COMMIT is how you keep it.

64
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:44,597
Until you commit, another session may not see your rows.

65
00:02:44,597 --> 00:02:46,972
That is a feature when you are still deciding.

66
00:02:46,972 --> 00:02:51,111
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS is for scripts you run twice.

67
00:02:51,111 --> 00:02:52,791
It is not a design tool.

68
00:02:52,791 --> 00:02:56,555
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS is for throwaway sandboxes.

69
00:02:56,555 --> 00:02:59,652
It is not how a store retires a customer list.

70
00:02:59,652 --> 00:03:02,750
INSERT can take a SELECT instead of VALUES.

71
00:03:02,750 --> 00:03:05,375
That copies rows from a staging table.

72
00:03:05,375 --> 00:03:07,597
Check the column order twice.

73
00:03:07,597 --> 00:03:11,944
UPDATE can use a join in some dialects to patch from another table.

74
00:03:11,944 --> 00:03:15,611
In SQLite you often write a subquery in SET.

75
00:03:15,611 --> 00:03:16,944
Still use WHERE.

76
00:03:16,944 --> 00:03:21,444
DELETE with a subquery can remove orphans you found on purpose.

77
00:03:21,444 --> 00:03:23,500
SELECT those orphans first.

78
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:26,638
Keep a backup file before a destructive script.

79
00:03:26,638 --> 00:03:29,152
A dump is cheaper than archaeology.

80
00:03:29,152 --> 00:03:31,902
Column types on ALTER vary by engine.

81
00:03:31,902 --> 00:03:37,111
SQLite will add a column and will not freely drop one in older versions.

82
00:03:37,111 --> 00:03:40,458
PostgreSQL will do more in one ALTER.

83
00:03:40,458 --> 00:03:43,625
Read the dialect before you promise a migration.

84
00:03:43,625 --> 00:03:47,680
Default values on a new column backfill old rows.

85
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:52,680
Choose the default as if it were a historical fact, because it becomes one.

86
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:53,944
Rename with care.

87
00:03:53,944 --> 00:03:57,375
Queries you have not found yet still use the old name.

88
00:03:57,375 --> 00:03:58,722
The verbs are few.

89
00:03:58,722 --> 00:04:01,291
The damage is large when WHERE is missing.

90
00:04:01,291 --> 00:04:04,083
That is why this lesson is slow on purpose.

91
00:04:04,083 --> 00:04:07,555
Shape, facts, correction, removal.

92
00:04:07,555 --> 00:04:10,208
Say which one you are doing before you type it.

93
00:04:10,208 --> 00:04:14,097
Table work and row work are two families of verbs.

94
00:04:14,097 --> 00:04:17,555
CREATE and ALTER and DROP change the object.

95
00:04:17,555 --> 00:04:21,708
INSERT and UPDATE and DELETE change the facts inside it.

96
00:04:21,708 --> 00:04:26,930
Mixing the families in your head is how DROP gets typed when DELETE was meant.

97
00:04:26,930 --> 00:04:32,013
CREATE TABLE starts with the name, then a list of columns in parentheses.

98
00:04:32,013 --> 00:04:34,694
Each column is a name, then a type.

99
00:04:34,694 --> 00:04:37,847
Lines in that list are separated by commas.

100
00:04:37,847 --> 00:04:42,138
The primary key is part of the contract, not a later wish.

101
00:04:42,266 --> 00:04:47,854
You can mark a column PRIMARY KEY on its line, or name the key in a separate line.

102
00:04:47,854 --> 00:04:52,896
Both mean: this value identifies the row, and it will not repeat.

103
00:04:52,896 --> 00:04:56,729
Types on CREATE are promises about what a cell may hold.

104
00:04:56,729 --> 00:05:00,229
A later INSERT that breaks the type is refused.

105
00:05:00,229 --> 00:05:02,785
That refusal is the point of the type.

106
00:05:02,785 --> 00:05:06,938
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS is for a script you run twice.

107
00:05:06,938 --> 00:05:10,952
It will not fix a table you already created with the wrong columns.

108
00:05:10,952 --> 00:05:16,118
DROP TABLE removes the object: name, columns, rows.

109
00:05:16,118 --> 00:05:20,563
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS is for a sandbox you are allowed to destroy.

110
00:05:20,563 --> 00:05:23,257
It is not how a store retires customers.

111
00:05:23,257 --> 00:05:27,771
INSERT lists columns, then VALUES, in the same order.

112
00:05:27,771 --> 00:05:30,243
A second INSERT is a second row.

113
00:05:30,243 --> 00:05:35,896
If you omit the column list, you are betting the table's column order never changes.

114
00:05:35,896 --> 00:05:37,313
Name the columns.

115
00:05:37,313 --> 00:05:42,327
INSERT can take a SELECT instead of VALUES when you copy from a staging table.

116
00:05:42,327 --> 00:05:44,966
Check that copy with a SELECT first.

117
00:05:44,966 --> 00:05:49,646
UPDATE names SET for the new value and WHERE for the rows that move.

118
00:05:49,646 --> 00:05:53,257
Without WHERE, every row takes the new value.

119
00:05:53,257 --> 00:05:56,591
That is the classic accident, and it is not rare.

120
00:05:56,591 --> 00:06:00,729
SELECT the same WHERE before you UPDATE, and look at the rows.

121
00:06:00,729 --> 00:06:01,868
Then change them.

122
00:06:01,868 --> 00:06:04,716
DELETE removes rows that match WHERE.

123
00:06:04,716 --> 00:06:08,660
Without WHERE, the table is empty and still exists.

124
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:12,577
That is different from DROP, which removes the table itself.

125
00:06:12,577 --> 00:06:19,007
TRUNCATE, in engines that have it, empties rows as a bulk wipe and may reset counters.

126
00:06:19,007 --> 00:06:21,660
Know which verb you are holding before you run it.

127
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:29,813
ALTER TABLE changes the contract after the table already has friends: queries, views, application code.

128
00:06:29,813 --> 00:06:32,327
ADD COLUMN is the usual growth.

129
00:06:32,327 --> 00:06:37,924
Existing rows get NULL unless you supply a default, and that default becomes history.

130
00:06:37,924 --> 00:06:40,591
DROP COLUMN throws the values away.

131
00:06:40,591 --> 00:06:43,160
There is no undo in the statement.

132
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:49,702
RENAME COLUMN breaks every query still using the old name, including ones you have not found yet.

133
00:06:49,702 --> 00:06:54,188
Dialects disagree on how much ALTER can do in one trip.

134
00:06:54,188 --> 00:06:59,354
SQLite will add a column readily and historically fought dropping one.

135
00:06:59,354 --> 00:07:02,896
PostgreSQL will do more in a single ALTER.

136
00:07:02,896 --> 00:07:06,341
Read the engine you are on before you promise a migration.

137
00:07:06,341 --> 00:07:13,716
BEGIN, then a change, then SELECT, then ROLLBACK, is how you practice without keeping the damage.

138
00:07:13,716 --> 00:07:15,313
COMMIT is how you keep it.

139
00:07:15,313 --> 00:07:19,479
Until you commit, another session may not see your rows.

140
00:07:19,479 --> 00:07:22,799
That isolation is a feature while you are still deciding.

141
00:07:22,799 --> 00:07:29,077
Shape, facts, correction, removal, later shape, destruction of the object.

142
00:07:29,077 --> 00:07:31,160
Say which one you are doing out loud.

143
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:33,285
Then type the verb that matches.

144
00:07:33,285 --> 00:07:38,216
The table stays a place you can trust when WHERE is present and DROP is rare.

145
00:07:38,216 --> 00:07:42,729
Table management commands: CREATE, ALTER, DROP.

146
00:07:42,729 --> 00:07:49,132
Data management commands: INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, and SELECT to verify.

147
00:07:49,132 --> 00:07:53,674
The lecture splits those families so you do not DROP when you meant DELETE.

148
00:07:53,674 --> 00:07:57,757
CREATE needs a name and a column list and usually a key.

149
00:07:57,757 --> 00:08:05,438
ALTER ADD is growth, ALTER DROP is loss, ALTER RENAME is a breaking change for callers.

150
00:08:05,438 --> 00:08:10,979
INSERT adds facts, UPDATE edits facts, DELETE removes facts.

151
00:08:10,979 --> 00:08:13,396
SELECT is how you look before you leap.

152
00:08:13,396 --> 00:08:20,368
Together they are the exact SQL you need to get a table installed and keep it honest, without a theory detour.

153
00:08:20,368 --> 00:08:27,257
Practice the families on a copy named customers_lab until the verbs feel different in your hands.

154
00:08:27,257 --> 00:08:30,091
Then use them on real names, slowly.

